  { n:24, summary:"Ordinances for the tabernacle came first: Israel supplied pure olive oil beaten for the light so the lamps could burn continually before the veil, tended by Aaron from evening to morning, and twelve fine-flour cakes were set in two rows on the pure table each sabbath as a memorial, later eaten by Aaron and his sons in the holy place. Then a fight in the camp led a son of an Israelitish woman by an Egyptian father to blaspheme the name of the Lord and curse. They put him in ward until the mind of the Lord was shown: all who heard him laid their hands on his head, and the congregation stoned him, for blasphemy brought death to stranger and native alike. The chapter also set the rule of like for like — he that killeth a man shall surely be put to death; eye for eye, tooth for tooth — with one manner of law for stranger and homeborn.",
    context:"The Lord spake unto Moses, ordering the lamp oil and showbread and then judging a case of blasphemy in the camp.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Leviticus 24:2", text:"Command the children of Israel, that they bring unto thee pure oil olive beaten for the light, to cause the lamps to burn continually." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 24:16", text:"And he that blasphemeth the name of the LORD, he shall surely be put to death, and all the congregation shall certainly stone him: as well the stranger, as he that is born in the land, when he blasphemeth the name of the LORD, shall be put to death." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 24:20", text:"Breach for breach, eye for eye, tooth for tooth: as he hath caused a blemish in a man, so shall it be done to him again." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's name is sacred, and justice must be even-handed — one law for the stranger and for the homeborn.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did the children of Israel supply so the lamps could burn continually?", choices:["Pure oil olive beaten for the light","Wax candles from Egypt","Dry juniper wood","Bulrushes soaked in tallow"], a:0, why:"Command the children of Israel, that they bring unto thee pure oil olive beaten for the light, to cause the lamps to burn continually." },
      { q:"What was the penalty for the man who blasphemed the name of the Lord?", choices:["Forty stripes save one","Banishment from the camp","A fine paid to the sanctuary","Death by stoning at the hands of all who heard him"], a:3, why:"Let all that heard him lay their hands upon his head, and let all the congregation stone him — blasphemy was punished with death." },
      { q:"How was justice to be applied to strangers in Israel?", choices:["Strangers were judged by their own laws","One manner of law applied to the stranger and to one of your own country","Strangers could appeal directly to the high priest","Strangers paid double restitution"], a:1, why:"Ye shall have one manner of law, as well for the stranger, as for one of your own country: for I am the LORD your God." }
    ] },
  { n:25, summary:"Speaking from mount Sinai, the Lord gave laws that reordered Israel's economy around rest and redemption. Every seventh year the land itself kept a sabbath: no sowing or pruning, with what grew of itself feeding people, servants, strangers, cattle, and beasts. After seven sabbaths of years, the trumpet of the jubilee sounded on the day of atonement to hallow the fiftieth year, proclaiming liberty throughout the land: every man returned to his possession and to his family. Land sales were priced by the years remaining until jubilee, for the land was the Lord's and could not be sold forever; poor Israelites were to be relieved without usury, and an Israelite who sold himself was to serve as a hired servant until jubilee, not as a bondservant, for the children of Israel were God's servants whom he brought out of Egypt. Walled-city houses and Levitical holdings carried special rules for redemption.",
    context:"The Lord spake unto Moses in mount Sinai, giving the sabbath year and jubilee laws for the land Israel was to possess.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Leviticus 25:4", text:"But in the seventh year shall be a sabbath of rest unto the land, a sabbath for the LORD: thou shalt neither sow thy field, nor prune thy vineyard." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 25:10", text:"And ye shall hallow the fiftieth year, and proclaim liberty throughout all the land unto all the inhabitants thereof: it shall be a jubilee unto you; and ye shall return every man unto his possession, and ye shall return every man unto his family." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 25:23", text:"The land shall not be sold for ever: for the land is mine; for ye are strangers and sojourners with me." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The land is the Lord's; liberty, restoration, and release from debt and bondage are woven into His design.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How often did the land itself keep a sabbath of rest?", choices:["Every month of harvest","In the seventh year, with no sowing or pruning","Every fiftieth day","Only in the jubilee year"], a:1, why:"But in the seventh year shall be a sabbath of rest unto the land, a sabbath for the LORD: thou shalt neither sow thy field, nor prune thy vineyard." },
      { q:"What two restorations did the jubilee year proclaim?", choices:["Cancellation of all feasts and tithes","Forgiveness of blasphemy and theft","Every man returned to his possession and to his family","Release of priests from their courses"], a:2, why:"It shall be a jubilee unto you; and ye shall return every man unto his possession, and ye shall return every man unto his family." },
      { q:"On what ground did the Lord forbid selling the land forever?", choices:["The land is mine; for ye are strangers and sojourners with me","The land was too rocky to sell","The Canaanites still claimed it","The priests held all the deeds"], a:0, why:"The land shall not be sold for ever: for the land is mine; for ye are strangers and sojourners with me." }
    ] },
  { n:26, summary:"Blessing and curse were set before Israel in sweeping terms. If they walked in God's statutes, he promised rain in due season, threshing that reached to the vintage, peace in the land, victory over enemies, fruitfulness, and his covenant established — above all, that he would set his tabernacle among them, walk among them, and be their God. If they refused to hearken, judgment escalated in waves of seven times: terror, disease, defeat, heavens like iron and earth like brass, wild beasts, sword, pestilence, famine so severe that ten women would bake bread in one oven, cities waste, sanctuaries desolate, and scattering among the heathen while the land enjoyed her sabbaths. Yet hope remained: if they confessed their iniquity and their uncircumcised hearts were humbled, God would remember his covenant with Jacob, Isaac, and Abraham — and even in the land of enemies he would not destroy them utterly or break his covenant.",
    context:"These statutes and judgments which the Lord made with the children of Israel in mount Sinai set blessings for obedience and curses for disobedience.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Leviticus 26:4", text:"Then I will give you rain in due season, and the land shall yield her increase, and the trees of the field shall yield their fruit." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 26:12", text:"And I will walk among you, and will be your God, and ye shall be my people." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 26:44", text:"And yet for all that, when they be in the land of their enemies, I will not cast them away, neither will I abhor them, to destroy them utterly, and to break my covenant with them: for I am the LORD their God." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Obedience brings God's nearness and peace; even in chastening, He remembers His covenant and invites a humble return.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many Israelite men were said to be able to put how many enemies to flight?", choices:["Two of you shall chase fifty","Ten shall chase a thousand","Five of you shall chase an hundred, and an hundred shall put ten thousand to flight","A thousand shall chase five"], a:2, why:"And five of you shall chase an hundred, and an hundred of you shall put ten thousand to flight: and your enemies shall fall before you by the sword." },
      { q:"What escalating phrase marked each new stage of judgment for disobedience?", choices:["Once and again will I visit you","Woe, woe unto this nation","Until three and four transgressions","I will punish you seven times more for your sins"], a:3, why:"If ye will not yet for all this hearken unto me, then I will punish you seven times more for your sins — the phrase repeats at each stage." },
      { q:"What promise remained even for Israel scattered among their enemies?", choices:["Their enemies would adopt their customs","If they confessed their iniquity and humbled their hearts, God would remember his covenant with Abraham","The priesthood would move to another nation","The law of Moses would be replaced"], a:1, why:"If they shall confess their iniquity, and their uncircumcised hearts be humbled, then will I remember my covenant with Jacob, and also my covenant with Isaac, and also my covenant with Abraham." }
    ] },
  { n:27, summary:"The book closes with laws about singular vows, when a person devoted a person, animal, house, or field to the Lord. Persons were valued by the priest's estimation in shekels of the sanctuary, set by age and sex — fifty shekels for a man in his prime, thirty for a woman — with the priest adjusting for poverty. A clean animal dedicated could not be swapped for another; if exchanged, both became holy. Houses and fields could be redeemed by adding a fifth part to the valuation, with field values reckoned by the years until jubilee; fields not redeemed became the priest's at jubilee. Firstlings could not be dedicated, since they already belonged to the Lord, and devoted things could not be sold or redeemed. The tithe of the land — seed, fruit, and every tenth animal passing under the rod — was holy unto the Lord and could be redeemed only with a fifth part added.",
    context:"The Lord spake unto Moses, giving laws for vows, valuations, and tithes to close the commandments given in mount Sinai.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Leviticus 27:2", text:"Speak unto the children of Israel, and say unto them, When a man shall make a singular vow, the persons shall be for the LORD by thy estimation." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 27:13", text:"But if he will at all redeem it, then he shall add a fifth part thereof unto thy estimation." },
      { ref:"Leviticus 27:30", text:"And all the tithe of the land, whether of the seed of the land, or of the fruit of the tree, is the LORD's: it is holy unto the LORD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Vows to God are sacred; what we dedicate to Him is valued, holy, and to be honored faithfully.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What was the valuation for a male between twenty and sixty years old who was the subject of a vow?", choices:["Fifty shekels of silver","Thirty shekels of silver","Ten shekels of silver","Five shekels of silver"], a:0, why:"Thy estimation shall be of the male from twenty years old even unto sixty years old... fifty shekels of silver, after the shekel of the sanctuary." },
      { q:"What belonged to the Lord already, so that it could not be sanctified by vow?", choices:["A field bought from a stranger","The houses of walled cities","The firstling of beasts, whether ox or sheep","The tithe of mint and cumin"], a:2, why:"Only the firstling of the beasts, which should be the LORD's firstling, no man shall sanctify it; whether it be ox, or sheep: it is the LORD's." },
      { q:"How much of the land's seed, fruit, and herds was declared holy unto the Lord?", choices:["A seventh part","A fifth part","Half in the jubilee year","The tithe — a tenth, including every tenth animal passing under the rod"], a:3, why:"All the tithe of the land... is the LORD's: it is holy unto the LORD; and of the herd or flock, the tenth, whatsoever passeth under the rod, shall be holy unto the LORD." }
    ] }
